Date: Sat, 1 Jan 1994 12:26:07 -0800 (PST)
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: ETHICS: Why I Value (Some) Cats Over (Most) Humans

I thought I'd add some fuel to the fire (flame bait) in the ongoing
charge that I am a people-hating sociopath.

Ray Cromwell writes:

>   Sure Jay! (my comments lately have been deliberately phrased to push
> buttons)

Ah, Ray admits to flame-baiting! So I guess I'm in good company.

Actually, neither Ray's comments, nor mine, nor Jay's are really flame
bait. In fact, important issues are being raised. 

Ray goes on to write:

>   For the record, I have 4 cats, I'm not an animal hater. However, I do
> not place the lives of animals on the same level. I'm humancentric, not
> ecocentric. (a 'specie-ist' as some leftists would call me)

I have two cats and I value them more highly than I value at least 99%
of the humans on this planet.

Abstract moral points about how "life is sacred" and "there is no
price on human life" are nonsense, to me. The value of human life
doesn't derive from logical principles of the universe, not does it
come from some Higher Power.

Morals are what we pay for. 

>From what I spend every year on my cats, in food and bet bills, I
could without any doubt "save" the lives of some peasants in various
Third World nations. That I don't means quite clearly I value at least
some cats (and other animals) over any of millions--even any of
billions--of people. Money talks, bullshit moralizing walks.

Now of course I don't expect starving Sudanese to Bengalis to see _my_
cats as anything other than food for the next six weeks, so I don't
assert some universal point about the absolute value of the lives of
my cats over their lives, etc.

This all fits with what I think is the natural, evolutionary approach,
where local interactions matter more than distant interactions and
where "Schelling points" of behavior, territorial boundaries, and
"morals" are mutually agreed upon.

Despite moralizing to the contrary, the fact that my fellow citizens
have not rounded up all the dogs and cats and turned them into canned
protein for shipment to Ethiopia, Sudan, Somalia, and Bangladesh is
tacit admission that such animals are, for whatever reason, more
valuable than are these people.

(In fairness, another important factor in the pets vs. peasants
equation is the realization that seizing people's pets as food, or to
stop the "pet food industry" from "wasting" billions of dollars, would
be highly intrusive into people's private lives, and would likely
affect societal happiness and productiveness. Thus, even in Stalinist
Russia and Maoist China, personal pets existed. In times of
starvation the pets vanished, of course.)

Morals are where we put our own time, effort, and money.

Sounds fair to me.

--Tim May



