Date: Sat, 1 Jan 1994 13:10:35 -0800 (PST)
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: Where to Live?

Simon! Levy raises the issue of what parts of the U.S. (or presumably
outside the U.S., too) are most suitable for Extropians to live in.

This is something I've been thinking about a lot lately, though I've
reached no firm conclusions.

> I sympathize with this viewpoint and would like to (re)start a thread on a
> related issue, namely, what is the best place for Extropians to live?  Many
> of us seem to have settled in northern California, but (earthquake peril

Obviously most Extropians in the Bay Area lived here first, _then_
joined the List (given the times involved, etc.). The Bay Area is a
target-rich environment for reruiting libertarians, computer folks,
Extropians, cryonicists, and other folks of that ilk. The music scene
here is huge, so is the arts scene. Both "Wired" and "Mondo 2000"
emanate (or choose other verb) from this region, and so on....no point
in trying to list the futurist-leaning attractions here.

By the way, the quake threat is almost certainly less than, say, the
hurricane and tornado threats faced by big chunks of the southern U.S.
I live less than 5 miles from the epicenter of the 1989 Loma Prieta
quake and my house sustained only minimal damage (knocked over
bookcases, contents of all kitchen cabinets dumped on floor, loss of
power for 2 days, a terrified cat). My sister went through the 1989
hurricane that hit Charleston, and that did as much or more damage,
roughly speaking. And hurricanes hit every couple of years.

> aside) I am concerned about living in CA, because it is supposed to be the
> most heavily regulated state in the country.  It seems like only a matter
> of time before the gun-grabbers use the violence in Los Angeles to impose
> state-wide restrictions (the way that the Connecticut legislature is trying
> to do here -- make the suburbs and boondocks pay for the problems of the
> cities).

Yes, and this worries me, too, But California is also home to many
extreme libertarians (translation: those who take the Constitution
seriously), right wingers, Birchers, Posse Comitatus members, armed
Korean store-owners, and generally uncooperative individualists.

Disarming this state will result in a lot of cop deaths, and many cops
are already making it clear they will not participate willingly in the
house-to-house searches which are being bandied about as the martial
law declaration will--if rumors are correct--call for.

> It seems to me that the southern U.S. has the optimal combination of low
> taxes and freedom from regulation (including gun control) that an Extropian
> would want.  My experience visiting my parents in North Carolina, where they've
> lived for the past six years, has convinced me that the quality of life there
> is better than anywhere else in the country.  I perceived a lower correlation 
> down there between race and poverty, and a consequent decrease of racial 
> tensions as compared with the Northeast and California.  (This despite the
> common perception of the south as the mother of all racism)  The downside is 
> being in the Bible Belt (my parents live a few miles from the Billy Grahm 
> Parkway), but I have not found that to be a problem.  I am a clean-cut 
> heterosexual white male, which could give me a false sense of safety, but 
> I am also Jewish, and my family has never experienced more than the normal 
> amount of anti-Semitism.

A problem here is that _national_ laws are taking precedence over
local laws, so that if Clinton and Company ban handguns, North
Carolina will be no more exempt than California. Ditto for other
repressive laws.

I can't speek about racism in the South, being a member of a minority
myself (white male, but not dead yet).

> In the Northeast, New Hampshire seems to be about the only state where
> libertarian attitudes are still taken seriously (they still have no state
> income tax).  

Nevada's the same way. I've toyed with the idea of moving to Reno or
thereabouts. 

> I bring this up in the context of Stuart's post because I am starting to
> believe that the smartest thing for Extropians to do may be to settle in
> a place where, for whatever reason, there are traditions consistent with
> some Extropian values.

Here we cut to the chase.

Most people will live where the jobs are. Period. For now, and for the
foreseeable future (a dangerous term on this List, so call it "for the
next 5 years at least"), this will mean the largish urban areas, such
as the Bay Area, Boston, New York, etc. And high-tech sites like
Austin, Research Triangle (in North Carolina...Simon!'s choice), etc.

Even our most vocal proponent of telecommuting and "perpetual
tourism," Duncan Frissell, chooses to live in New Jersey. Now I'm not
sure why he chooses to live in New Jersey, and I'm sure there are some
nice places in the country there, but images of Perth Amboy and Newark
come to mind when I think of New Jersey. Restrictive gun laws are
almost the least of their worries.

So where to live?

Jobs, jobs, jobs. Or, in the future, income, income, income. Most
folks will pick places for these reasons first, then will try to
locally optimize by picking neighborhoods, dwellings, etc.

Only a very few can or will pick the globally best place to live and
then worry about income afterwards. For those who wish to, there are
books like "Retirement Places Rated" which give climate, cultural
events, sports activities, home prices, cost of living, etc.

(Someday there will be custom agents which sort through large
databases of regions and allow gun-lovers, for example, to optimize.
Sounds like a good idea for a small business. If AMIX still existed,
and the target audience used it, this could be done on AMIX. As it is,
setting it up and then advertising in specialty magazines (guns,
fishing, cultural, etc.). Maybe someone out there will do it.)

Some random thoughts on this:

- a lot of my friends live in the Bay Area, the result of spending the
last 20 years here (which is why I moved to the coastal town of Santa
Cruz when I retired....a nice locale, with a big university and lots
of activities, but close enough to get over to the Valley on
weekends).

- house prices are indeed high here, but so are salaries. The engineer
being paid $3000 a month in Raleigh-Durham is likely paid $4000 a month
in Palo Alto, which goes some distance toward making up for the house
price differential.

- Areas in the U.S. that intrigue me now are Santa Fe, Reno, New
Orleans, and Charleston. 

- Having relatively warm weather is important. Having a university is
a big plus.

- I also periodically think about leaving the U.S., for reasons I won't
go into now. 

An interesting Extropian topic.

Anyway, I'll stop for now. 


--Tim May



