Subject: the extropian pan-opticon

Why put criminals in prisons at all?

Meet Hilly Worton, the convict of the 21st century. He's on his way to the
grocery, to buy some food. He has several miniature transponders under his
skin, which allow the authorities to track his every move (if they so
wish). When he goes out in public, a device he wears at his neck makes an
audible warning - "crook! crook!" It's annoying, so he doesn't like to go
out. Isn't that tattoo they put on his forehead enough?

And it's not as if he feels much like commiting larceny from a wheelchair.
Can't wait to get his legs back. Maybe he should have chosen prison, or
torture. They say it's harmless.

He's lost. Sometimes the passivity drugs he takes make him confused. He
asks directions from the guy at the newsstand cleaning his Glock 27.

He buys food. If he keeps at his present rate of income it shouldn't be
long before he can pay off the fine and get all these impediments removed.
New legs, no transponders, maybe even pay to get rid of the tattoo.

Better make some good contacts with the authorities though, show them he
knows what's what, or this could all happen again. After all, he's
innocent.

(I wrote this as a distopia, but I feel compelled to point out that none of
it is obviously worse than the status quo.)

Dave                       tburns@mason1.gmu.edu (T. David Burns)



