Ray Cromwell writes --

> I believe the earth is quite robust...If ecosystems
> were that fragile, they would have collapsed long ago.

The EARTH is robust, but ecosystems collapse all the time.
Humans are not likely to extinguish all life on earth --
there are life forms which can (and do) flourish in
'black smokers' at temperatures well above the boiling
point of water at atmospheric pressure (although slightly
below the boiling point of water at their ambient pressure).

But I want to LIVE here.  It is quite possible that humans
could disturb the ecosystem to the point where it would
re-establish dynamic equilibrium about an attractor
quite hostile to human life.  It is believed that the
atmosphere has completely changed composition at least
twice; I would rather not try breathing methane and
ammonia.  Even if we didn't push things so far that we
could not live here, it would be easy to push things to
the point where I wouldn't like it very much.  In fact,
we have already done so, in my opinion, to most arable
areas.  The population densities in most livable areas
are way too high for me to want to live there.

But do I have any idea how to protect the ecosystem
without violating individual (objectively nonexistent,
but useful fiction) rights? Nope.
