Singapore, I tried to capture the "flavor" of that viewpoint as
manifest by the Chinese Singaporeans.  The following is an
excerpt from a letter I wrote on 28 November 1991.  I hope you
enjoy it.

                            *   *   *

Animals are a part of Singapore.  They fall into four general
categories:  wildlife, vermin, pets and food.  Since this is a
predominantly Chinese country, all four categories overlap
extensively.

WILDLIFE--The Singaporeans have been extremely efficient in
dealing with wildlife--it has all but been eliminated.  No more
jaguars, monkeys nor snakes.  Gone.  The remaining exceptions are
birds, lizards and newts.  They are tolerated because they eat
category number two, vermin.

VERMIN--Even the vermin-eating wildlife is in short supply
because the vermin, themselves, are endangered species.  I have
not seen one mosquito in tropical Singapore (S$50 fine for
*every* larvae found on your property).  Ditto, for flies.  The
two cockroaches that thought was there apartment, left when we
moved in.  (One giant, hundred-year-old cockroach left via the
toilet after I brained him with a shoe.)  Rats, mice?  Maybe, but
haven't seen any.  I did see two tame mice living underwater in
an exhibit at the Singapore Science Centre.  They were dry and
comfortable in an air-filled chamber with walls that allowed
oxygen in, but kept the water out.  In some ways, oh-too-true
metaphor for life in Singapore, itself.

PETS--This includes dogs and cats, but damn few of either.  The
cats all have short, bulbous tails.  My associate says they are a
special strain of cats like the tailless Manx.  I'm not so sure.
You always see a lot of lop-tailed cats outside Chinese
restaurants, if you get my drift.

FOOD--This includes chickens, cows, pigs, lamb, squid, crabs,
octopus, prawn, every kind of fish . . . and all of the above.

                            *   *   *


